  "slug": "activation-line-c0a8",
  "summary": "Single-cell transcriptomics now pairs cell-state inference with tissue microenvironment context across human disease and aging datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is single-cell and multiomic profiling used to define cell states together with the microenvironments they occupy. Studies span skeletal muscle, where S1P-secreting stem cells are proposed to drive niche remodeling during regeneration, cardiac lymphatic endothelium remodeled by aging and cardiovascular disease, and colon fibroblasts mapped with spatial validation against cancer-associated subsets. Vascular and immune niches recur, including an aging-associated ETS1-MYO1B vascular niche, TREM2-dependent myeloid programming in pulmonary hypertension, and stromal control of neutrophil phenotypes in ulcerative colitis. What is comparatively new is the explicit linkage of state-level inference to tissue niche context, with spatial anchoring in some items. Most conclusions remain candidate-level and hypothesis-generating, and the review of VSMC surface signatures addresses related questions from a translational angle.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-26"
}
